","split_unverified":true},{"surface":"எனவே","parts":[{"text":"என","lemma":"என","gloss":"thus"},{"text":"ஏ","lemma":"ஏ","gloss":"indeed"}],"gloss":"therefore"}]}],"literal":"Women with curved earrings having adorned with garlands / having drunk fiber-filtered liquor to remove the shivering cold / the horse of strong gallop that surpasses the action of the wind / you hasten to saddle, you indeed! Yesterday, / 'Together with the younger brother of the one who escaped my elder brother, / tomorrow I will fight a battle,' having said, / without even going to feed his small stomach, the strong horse / he will ride, they say, greatly. Having heard that, / the king of victorious war with the victory-yielding drum, / his shining, vast army camp shivered, / because what he said will not become two (will not fail).","translation":"Women wearing curved earrings adorned the horse with garlands. To dispel the shivering cold, the warrior drank fiber-filtered liquor. You hasten to saddle this horse of strong gallop, whose speed surpasses the wind! Yesterday, he declared: 'Tomorrow, I shall wage battle against the younger brother of the one who escaped my elder brother.' Without even pausing to feed his own small stomach, they say he will ride his strong horse. Hearing this, the victorious king of the triumphant war-drum trembled in his vast, shining army camp, knowing that the warrior's word never fails.","modern":"வளைந்த குண்டலங்களை அணிந்த பெண்கள் மாலைகளைச் சூட்ட, நடுங்கும் குளிரைப் போக்குவதற்காக நார் கொண்டு வடிக்கப்பட்ட கள்ளைப் பருகிவிட்டு, காற்றின் வேகத்தையும் மிஞ்சும் வலிமையான குதிரையைச் சேணமிட்டு ஆயத்தப்படுத்த நீ விரைந்து கொண்டிருக்கிறாய்! நேற்று அவன், 'என் அண்ணனிடமிருந்து தப்பி ஓடியவனின் தம்பியோடு நாளை நான் போர் செய்வேன்' என்று கூறினான். தன் சிறிய வயிற்றுக்கு உணவளிக்கக் கூடச் செல்லாமல், அவன் தன் வலிமையான குதிரையைச் செலுத்திப் போருக்கு வருவான் என்று பலரும் கூறுகின்றனர். அதைக் கேட்டதும், வெற்றி முரசைக் கொண்ட போர்க்குணமிக்க பகை வேந்தனின் விளங்கும் பெரிய பாசறை நடுங்கியது. ஏனெனில், அவன் கூறிய வார்த்தை ஒருபோதும் பொய்க்காது, மாறாது.","explanation":"This poem belongs to the Tumpai tiṇai (battle/warfare) and the theme of kutirai maṟam (the valor of the warrior's horse). An observer or bard addresses the horse-keeper who is hurriedly preparing the warrior's horse for battle. The warrior has taken a solemn vow to defeat the younger brother of the enemy who had previously escaped his elder brother. He is so consumed by this vow that he refuses to eat and prepares to ride out immediately. Hearing of his absolute determination, the enemy king's vast army camp trembles in fear because they know the warrior's word never fails. The imagery of the swift horse, faster than the wind, and the warrior drinking filtered liquor to ward off the cold highlights the intense preparation and fierce resolve of the hero.","model":"gemini-3.5-flash"}}